\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton, CEO of the Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia, gave Gerald Hardman, Commissioner of the Department of Traffic Movement and Property Security, a single brief:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qc\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f2\i\fs22 Locate and open with all due urgency, and using any resources necessary,\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>the HIBReLabs facility containing the Parramatta Virus.
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The security risk of freeing any Professor Hermann or Doctor Anstey after many decades\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Many, many decades,\'94 Gerry interrupted, then instantly regretted it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who knows what hidden secrets, what long-buried dirt they could use for leverage.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s impossible anyone sealed in the labs could still be alive, your Serene Corporateness.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can\'92t risk any Teenage Mutant Ninja Nerds escaping and spreading the Parramatta Virus. Do you understand the implications?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The entire health industry depends on drip-feeding remedies for decades. An instant cure to anything\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, everything. Including ageing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shut up, Hardman.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m agreeing with you, sir.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shut up especially when you\'92re agreeing with me. You can\'92t lick my arse through a tablet camera, even if I drop my daks and sit on the screen. Just tell me you understand. The economy needs an underclass with long, drawn-out illnesses. Horrible ones. Expensive ones.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I understand, sir.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good. Now get me that Parramatta Virus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry\'92s screen went dead. \'93The TMinusVirus, your Serene Salamander Face,\'94 he murmured at the blank glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A minute later, Gerry received a formal message. In summary: if he failed, every financial detail of his would be delivered to The High Tea Lady of the Ulyssian Tax Office. He\'92d be bankrupt.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Gerry set his considerable team in motion. They began with what everyone ranked as the lowest-risk prospect: a small facility dug into the Western Desert\'92s forgiving sedimentary rock, perfect for beta-testing their excavation techniques. Out here, any disturbance was fair game\'97noise, dust, loose earth, even a spontaneous geyser could erupt and nobody credible would complain. Out there, they could risk a full-blown zombie apocalypse of long-lost scientists and still keep it contained.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The Western Desert site delivered no virus, but it did yield a tax-you addendum: tucked away in an office was documentation on another dozen HIBReLabs. Gerry tossed caution to the rock saws and diamond drills and ordered all geographically easy sites cracked open three at a time. As the project grew, he reviewed the army of equipment with a secret flush of pride; this was serious resource command, serious budget.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But as the weeks wore on, dread gnawed through Gerry\'92s optimism. Every bit of experience screamed that the true virus site would be the very one he least wanted breached: the facility buried under the most expensive Airborne towers in town, their tentacular pylons plunging through the Layer for anchorage in the city\'92s inner core, the district called the Pale. The Pale was infamous: poorest neighbourhood, Layer thick as gravy, weather always set to \'93toxic.\'94 Causing explosions under those towers was a recipe for public relations collapse, if not whole-building catastrophe. For now, Gerry played it safe, dismissing the worst-case dig while working through easier targets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not that those were a walk in the park. A clear pattern appeared: every lab was sunk at least fifty metres down, under solid strata\'97mostly uncut volcanic rock or dense conglomerate\'97sealed beneath domes of reinforced, ultra-high-performance concrete ten metres thick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Labour was slow and exacting. The deeper they bored, the more delicate the process; if they triggered a cave-in, the archives and offices below might be lost, along with any clues to the virus\'92s real resting place.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Over weeks, they opened and combed through nearly all the labs. Each successful breach brought fresh evidence of yet more sites: a scavenger hunt with more steps at every turn. Gerry threw more resources at the problem until only two candidates remained. One was the facility in the Pale that Gerry had been avoiding. The other was shrouded in ambiguity, known only from cryptic financial paperwork, always surfacing in files mislaid by other facility accountants. That the second one might house any HIBReLabs work was borderline unthinkable: the supposed address placed it inside, or beneath, Jamison Tower, right in the city\'92s financial heart.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No more stalling. It was time for Gerry to reckon with the Pale site\'97likely home to the viruses, and, if worst came to worst, to living, centuries-old nerd fossils.
